<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ajmer</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ajayameru</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ajmer is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, among the Aravalli hills. Founded as Ajayameru by the Chahamana king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajayaraja-ii">Ajayaraja II</a> in the early 12th century, it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a> before the Ghurid conquest of 1192. It is famous for the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Moinuddin Chishti, a major pilgrimage centre; under British rule it formed the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer-merwara">Ajmer-Merwara</a> and it joined Rajasthan in 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajmer.jpg" alt="Ajmer" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SUDHIR UPADHYAY JI · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ADHAI_DIN_KA_JHONPRA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ajmer lies in a valley of the Aravalli Range in the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, on the lower slopes of Taragarh Hill. Its name comes from Ajaya Meru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for invincible hill, given by a Chahamana ruler, either Ajayaraja I or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajayaraja-ii">Ajayaraja II</a>, and referring to Taragarh Hill. To the northwest the Nagapathar Range protects it from the spread of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a>. Because of its central position in the state it is called the Heart of Rajasthan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The sources disagree on the founding. A prashasti issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vigraharaja-iv">Vigraharaja IV</a> states that Ajayadeva, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajayaraja-ii">Ajayaraja II</a>, moved his residence to Ajmer, and the earliest mention of the city's name is in a text copied in 1113 CE, so Ajmer existed before then. A later text says an earlier king built the fort that became Taragarh, and a historian, R. B. Singh, finds this likely since inscriptions of the 8th century have been found there. Singh suggests that Ajayaraja II expanded the town, built palaces and moved the Chahamana capital there from Shakambhari. The Ghurids later annexed Ajmer, and it was later returned to Gaur Rajput rulers on condition of tribute. By the 15th century Mewar had captured it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> conquered Ajmer in 1556 and made it capital of the Ajmer Subah. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> made frequent pilgrimages to the dargah of Moinuddin Chishti, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahanara-begum">Jahanara Begum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shikoh</a>, children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, were born there in 1614 and 1615. Mughal patronage waned by the early 18th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scindias">Scindias</a> conquered the city in 1752, and the British gained authority in 1818. Under British rule it was headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer-merwara">Ajmer-Merwara Province</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayo-college">Mayo College</a> was founded in 1875 for the sons of chiefs and nobles. After independence in 1947 Ajmer continued as a separate state until 1956, when it was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ajmer is the administrative headquarters of its district and division, and had 542,321 inhabitants in the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The Ajmer Sharif Dargah, the shrine of Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti at the foot of Taragarh Hill, has white marble buildings around two courtyards and draws many pilgrims. Other sights include Taragarh Fort, the mosque known as Adhai Din Ka Jhonpra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s fort, now a museum, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ana-sagar-lake">Ana Sagar Lake</a>. The temple town of Pushkar lies a few kilometres away.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ajmer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many of the Rajput dynasties that dominated northern India in the tenth to twelfth centuries, including the Chandelas of Khajuraho, the Paramaras of Malwa and the Chahamanas who later ruled Delhi and <b>Ajmer</b>, rose as Pratihara feudatories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Act of 1956 completed the process: from 1 November 1956 Abu and Delwara, the Part C state of <b>Ajmer</b>, formerly the British province of Ajmer-Merwara, and the Sunel Tappa enclave were added, the Sironj subdivision went to Madhya Pradesh, and the office of Rajpramukh was abolished in favour…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Islamic law, sat as qazis, from the district judge to the chief qazi who travelled with the imperial camp, and Sufi shaikhs kept shrines that emperors themselves visited: Akbar prayed at the tomb of Moinuddin Chishti at <b>Ajmer</b> and built Fatehpur Sikri beside the shrine of Salim Chishti.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Ajayaraja founded <b>Ajmer</b> around 1113 and made it the capital, and Arnoraja built the Ana Sagar lake.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-shakambhari">Chahamanas of Shakambhari</a></li><li><blockquote>Kishangarh was founded in 1611 by Kishan Singh, a son of Raja Udai Singh of Marwar, who received the territory near <b>Ajmer</b> from the Mughal emperor Jahangir.</blockquote><a href="/state/kishangarh-state">Kishangarh State</a></li></ul></section>
